---

<Layout
  title={`Minerals and Energy Resources | CBSE Class 10 Geography | ${provider.name}`}
  description="CBSE Class 10 Geography Chapter 5 study notes on Minerals and Energy Resources. Classification of minerals (metallic, non-metallic, energy), mode of occurrence, major iron ore belts, bauxite, mica, conventional energy (coal, petroleum, natural gas, hydel/thermal) and non-conventional energy (nuclear, solar, wind, biogas, tidal) with 20 board exam quiz questions."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Minerals and Energy Resources
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Social Science · Geography Chapter 5</span>
      <h1>Minerals and Energy Resources</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Uncover the treasures beneath the Earth's crust: mineral occurrence geology, iron ore belts, energy fuels from Gondwana coal to offshore petroleum, and India's transition to solar and wind energy.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Mineral Geology, Distribution &amp; Energy Systems</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>⛏️ Mode of Occurrence of Minerals</h3>
          <p class="formula">Where are Minerals Found?</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>1. In Igneous and Metamorphic Rocks:</strong> Minerals occur in cracks, crevices, faults, or joints. Smaller occurrences are called <em>veins</em> and larger ones are called <em>lodes</em> (e.g. copper, zinc, lead, tin).</li>
            <li><strong>2. In Sedimentary Rocks:</strong> Minerals occur in beds or layers formed as a result of deposition, accumulation, and concentration in horizontal strata (e.g. coal, iron ore, gypsum, potash salt).</li>
            <li><strong>3. Decomposition of Surface Rocks:</strong> Residual mass of weathered material containing ores after soluble constituents are washed away (e.g. Bauxite).</li>
            <li><strong>4. Alluvial Deposits (Placer Deposits):</strong> Found in sands of valley floors and base of hills; not corroded by water (e.g. gold, silver, tin, platinum).</li>
            <li><strong>5. Ocean Waters &amp; Beds:</strong> Common salt, magnesium, bromine; ocean beds rich in manganese nodules.</li>
            <li><strong>Rat-Hole Mining:</strong> In Meghalaya (Jowai, Cherrapunji), coal and limestone mined by tribal families by digging long narrow tunnels (declared illegal by the National Green Tribunal).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🔩 Ferrous &amp; Non-Ferrous Minerals</h3>
          <p class="formula">Iron Ore, Manganese, Bauxite, Mica</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Iron Ore (Backbone of Industrial Development):</strong>
              <br/>• <strong>Magnetite:</strong> Finest iron ore with up to 70% iron content; excellent magnetic qualities, valuable in electrical industry.
              <br/>• <strong>Hematite:</strong> Most important industrial iron ore; 50–60% iron content.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Major Iron Ore Belts in India:</strong>
              <br/>1. <em>Odisha-Jharkhand belt:</em> High-grade hematite in Badampahar mines (Mayurbhanj/Kendujhar) and Gua/Noamundi (Singhbhum).
              <br/>2. <em>Durg-Bastar-Chandrapur belt:</em> Bailadila range in Bastar (Chhattisgarh) produces super high-grade hematite exported to Japan/South Korea via Visakhapatnam port.
              <br/>3. <em>Ballari-Chitradurga-Chikkamagaluru-Tumakuru belt:</em> Kudremukh mines (100% export unit; transported as slurry through pipeline to Mangaluru port).
              <br/>4. <em>Maharashtra-Goa belt:</em> Exported via Marmagao port.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Non-Ferrous:</strong> Bauxite (aluminum ore; Odisha is largest producer; Panchpatmali deposits in Koraput), Copper (Khetri in Rajasthan, Balaghat in MP, Singhbhum in Jharkhand).</li>
            <li><strong>Mica:</strong> Indispensable in electric and electronic industries due to dielectric strength, low power loss, and resistance to high voltage. Koderma-Gaya-Hazaribagh belt of Jharkhand leads.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🔥 Conventional Energy Sources</h3>
          <p class="formula">Coal, Petroleum, Natural Gas &amp; Electricity</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Coal:</strong> Most abundantly available fossil fuel in India.
              <br/>• <em>Peat:</em> Low carbon, high moisture, low heating capacity.
              <br/>• <em>Lignite:</em> Low-grade brown coal, soft with high moisture (Neyveli in Tamil Nadu; used for power generation).
              <br/>• <em>Bituminous:</em> Most popular commercial coal; buried deep and subjected to increased temperatures; high heating value.
              <br/>• <em>Anthracite:</em> Highest quality hard coal (&gt; 80% carbon).
              <br/>• <em>Geological Ages:</em> Gondwana coal (over 200 million years old; Damodar valley: Jharia, Raniganj, Bokaro) and Tertiary coal (55 million years old; Assam, Meghalaya, Arunachal).
            </li>
            <li><strong>Petroleum:</strong> 63% of India's petroleum comes from <strong>Mumbai High</strong>, 18% from Gujarat (Ankleshwar), and 16% from Assam (Digboi is oldest oil field; Naharkatiya, Moran-Hugrijan).</li>
            <li><strong>Natural Gas:</strong> Clean energy fuel with low carbon emissions. Krishna-Godavari basin, Mumbai High, Gulf of Cambay. HVJ pipeline (Hazira-Vijaipur-Jagdishpur) connects western fields to northern fertilizer and power plants.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>☀️ Non-Conventional Energy Sources</h3>
          <p class="formula">Renewable, Eco-friendly &amp; Decentralized</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Nuclear / Atomic Energy:</strong> Obtained by altering structure of atoms (fission of Uranium and Thorium). Uranium found in Jharkhand and Aravalli range; Thorium found in <strong>Monazite sands of Kerala</strong>. Major plants: Tarapur (Maharashtra), Rawatbhata (Rajasthan), Kalpakkam (TN), Narora (UP), Kiga (Karnataka), Kakrapara (Gujarat).</li>
            <li><strong>Solar Energy:</strong> India is a tropical country with vast solar potential. Thar desert has enormous solar parks. Photovoltaic technology converts sunlight directly into electricity.</li>
            <li><strong>Wind Power:</strong> Largest wind farm cluster located in <strong>Tamil Nadu from Nagarcoil to Madurai</strong>. Jaisalmer in Rajasthan is also well known for effective wind energy.</li>
            <li><strong>Biogas:</strong> Produced from shrubs, farm waste, animal and human waste. 'Gobar gas plants' in rural areas provide clean cooking gas and high-quality manure.</li>
            <li><strong>Tidal &amp; Geothermal:</strong> Gulf of Khambhat and Gulf of Kutch in Gujarat have 900 MW tidal potential. Geothermal projects located in <strong>Parvati valley near Manikaran</strong> (Himachal Pradesh) and <strong>Puga Valley in Ladakh</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

